  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper aims to investigate whether 3-aminopropyl-triethoxysilane (APTES) retards early cement hydration by adsorbing onto Ca3SiO5 surfaces and producing a shielding effect, using ab-initio metadynamics simulations with experimental corroboration to test whether adsorption changes spontaneous dissolution of surface calcium into a less spontaneous or non-spontaneous process, lowers calcium-ion concentration, and slows hydration.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When silane coupling agents may inhibit early cement hydration but the nanoscale mechanism remains unclear, atomistic simulation combined with experimental corroboration is an appropriate strategy for investigating how APTES affects Ca3SiO5 hydration and dissolution."];
  "Node_4_0" [label="(4,0,0) The knowledge is that SCAs typically have a bifunctional structure consisting of organic and inorganic components."];
  "Node_4_1" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, in acidic or alkaline solutions, the alkoxy groups undergo hydrolysis to form hydroxyl groups."];
  "Node_4_2" [label="(6,0,0) Commonly, these hydroxyl groups can then participate in condensation reactions with C-S-H."];
  "Node_4_3" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given alkoxy groups hydrolyze and commonly condense with C-S-H, therefore SCAs chemically bond with cementitious materials."];
  "Node_4_4" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given SCAs chemically bond with cementitious materials and have a bifunctional structure, therefore the chemical structure of SCAs enables their bonding and effectiveness."];
  "Node_A2" [label="(0,0,0) If the inclusion of SCAs introduces adverse effects like the inhibition of initial cement hydration, then there remain gaps in understanding the mechanisms of silane-induced delayed hydration."];
  "Node_A4" [label="(0,0,0) If there remain gaps in understanding the mechanisms of silane-induced delayed hydration, then the precise impact of silane on clinker dissolution during the induction stage warrants further exploration."];
  "Node_B2" [label="(0,0,0) Commonly, delaying the emergence of the hydration peak indicates the inhibition of initial hydration."];
  "Node_C1" [label="(2,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the demand is increasing, therefore enhancing the surface hydrophobicity of concrete is crucial for protecting the underlying substrate."];
  "Node_C2" [label="(7,0,0) The knowledge is that the organo-functional groups provided by SCAs impart hydrophobicity to concrete."];
  "Node_C3" [label="(3,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given hydrophobicity is crucial and SCAs impart hydrophobicity, therefore one effective strategy to improve the impermeability of concrete is the incorporation of silane coupling agents (SCAs)."];
  "Node_D1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, the demand for higher-durability concrete that can withstand ion corrosion is increasing."];
  "Node_D2" [label="(0,0,0) If the demand for higher-durability concrete is increasing, then enhancing surface hydrophobicity is crucial for protecting the substrate."];
  "Node_E0" [label="(0,0,0) If the chemical structure of SCAs enables their bonding and effectiveness, then the hydrophobic groups of silanes negatively impact silicate polymerization and hydrogen bond formation, retarding hydration."];
  "Node_P2" [label="(30,0,0) The knowledge is that atomistic simulations have emerged as an alternative method to provide valuable insights into the nanoscale mechanisms of the inhibition of SCAs on cement hydration."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Given that the impact of silane on clinker dissolution during the induction stage warrants further exploration, and atomistic simulations can provide nanoscale mechanistic insight into SCA inhibition of cement hydration, the study is motivated to combine ab initio simulations with experimental corroboration to investigate the impact of APTES on early Ca3SiO5 hydration at the nanoscale."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) Given the stated conditional premise, the bonding-enabled chemical structure of SCAs makes it plausible to consider hydrophobic silane groups as contributors to retarded hydration through effects on silicate polymerization and hydrogen-bond formation, rather than proving this as a direct consequence of bifunctionality alone."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because SCAs are proposed for incorporation into concrete and their hydrophobic silane groups are treated as plausible contributors to hydration retardation, incorporating SCAs can plausibly delay the emergence of the hydration peak."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) If incorporating SCAs can delay the emergence of the hydration peak, and such a delay commonly indicates inhibition of initial hydration, then SCAs can introduce adverse effects such as inhibition of initial cement hydration."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Because SCAs can introduce adverse effects such as inhibition of initial cement hydration, there remain gaps in understanding the mechanisms of silane-induced delayed hydration."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Because gaps remain in understanding silane-induced delayed hydration, the precise impact of silane on clinker dissolution during the induction stage warrants further exploration."];
